qx|A3|st||rh||ah|Board 3 IMPs   -   April 17, 2024|pn|hondo717,Honeydog3,Rustgi65,danpsych|
md|1SA5432HJTDQ832CQ8,SK98HAK765DJ654C9,ST6HQ943DKTCAJT65,SQJ7H82DA97CK7432|sv|e|
mb|P|mb|1H|mb|P|mb|1N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I agree with the 1N bid, playing 2/1 game forcing (where 2@C would be a game force). The way you determine whether or not you have a game forcing hand after partner opens is if you have a hand that meets
the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule Of 20^*N|CP||FP||at|, you should force to game. But the East hand only counts to 19 (11 HCP and 8 cards in 2 longest suits).|
PG| ***** 47 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Normally, I play a convention here that I really like but is rarely played. It is called ^*BDelayed DONT^*n. In this convention over any opponent's NT bid, whether it is a 1N opener, 1N response, or 1N rebid all bids are DONT. So, since I have 5 spades and 4 diamonds I could bid 2@D to show diamonds and a major. But my hand is a bit flawed in that it is 5-4-2-2 and it has 2 dubious honors in clubs and hearts.|
PG| ***** 48 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Had my hand been say AQxxx xx QJxx xx which is the same number of HCP but the honors are much better placed (e.g. all my honors are in my 2 longest suits, which is very desirable) I would bid 2@D. Points matter a bit, but you can do it with a relatively weak hand if you have good distribution and placement of high cards.|
PG| ***** 49 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^B@2^*BA Kibitzer: ^*N Is 1NT semi forcing here?|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I play forcing particularly since East is unpassed hand but even if it were semi-forcing West still should bid 2@D. |
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


mb|p|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is not correct. You only pass 1N semi-forcing with a balanced hand and West certainly does not have a balanced hand. If you have a 4-card suit you bid it over 1N. If for no other reason than East might have a 3-card limit raise.|
PG| ***** 52 ***** |


ub||
PG| ***** 53 ***** |


mb|2D|mb|P?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East's choices are 2@H (my choice) or 2N (reasonable). 2N shows 10 or 11 points. In general you should avoid rebidding 2N after a 1N response.|
PG| ***** 54 ***** |


mb|2H|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 55 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|lb|*CLead for North by clicking that card on the right.^^ST^St6|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|North should lead the @S10--high from doubleton. I don't love leading doubletons but given the auction, a spade looks like the best lead from North's hand.|
